    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Detailed field investigation of the Waterpocket monocline in southern Utah reveals that faulting patterns in even relatively simple folds exhibit complex patterns. Within the Navajo Formation, the density and kinematics of some fault sets are found to correlate with along-strike bends in the monocline axis. These bends may result from segmentation of the basement faults coring the monoclines. Faults within the Navajo Formation near these bends record a strong component of lateral slip that probably results from the folding of the unit about a vertical, as well as horizontal, axis near the bends. The spatial density of faulting is observed to decrease with increasing distance from bends in the fold axis; however, the perturbation of the stress field caused by such bends may extend several kilometers and influence fault development even within straight segments of the fold.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
